WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:01.880
Film Courage: So you say that the B.M.O.C.

00:00:01.880 --> 00:00:07.430
You say the B stands for one time marker,
M another, O another and on.

00:00:07.430 --> 00:00:12.020
Peter Russell, screenwriter/script doctor/instructor:
Yes, 30, 60, 90, 110 page in a script (a movie

00:00:12.020 --> 00:00:13.020
script).

00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:14.100
Film Courage: Okay to play the devil’s advocate
here.

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:19.070
We sometimes see these comments (usually they
come in late at night) which are "This is

00:00:19.070 --> 00:00:22.090
why Hollywood writes formulaic crap."

00:00:22.090 --> 00:00:23.249
Peter: Yes!

00:00:23.249 --> 00:00:25.660
Film Courage: How do we contend that?

00:00:25.660 --> 00:00:28.599
How do we say “Well, maybe Hollywood is
not for you.”

00:00:28.599 --> 00:00:30.380
But what would be your answer to someone?

00:00:30.380 --> 00:00:34.010
Peter: Take a show that I would think would
be the new Robert Altman who is a guy named

00:00:34.010 --> 00:00:35.010
Glover.

00:00:35.010 --> 00:00:39.890
He’s a guy that is writing a show right
now that is called ATLANTA.

00:00:39.890 --> 00:00:45.620
Now ATLANTA is now the hippest half-hour show
on television.

00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:47.000
Glover is an African-American dude.

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:55.040
He’s been an actor in several big shows
but now he’s got his own show.

00:00:55.040 --> 00:01:00.840
This guy gives us a world that we’ve never
seen before which is the world of middle…kind

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:05.620
of lower middle-class African American Atlanta.

00:01:05.620 --> 00:01:07.820
Now Atlanta is a cool city.

00:01:07.820 --> 00:01:13.729
And there is a big African American culture
that is part of Atlanta.

00:01:13.729 --> 00:01:17.790
I would say say to you, you probably don’t
know too much about that world, right?

00:01:17.790 --> 00:01:18.790
Film Courage: I don’t.

00:01:18.790 --> 00:01:20.439
Peter: I certainly don’t either.

00:01:20.439 --> 00:01:23.620
He shows you and that’s one of the great
things there are nine questions that I ask

00:01:23.620 --> 00:01:25.579
you when I’m working on a television show.

00:01:25.579 --> 00:01:28.350
And one of them is “Are you showing me a
world I haven’t seen before, because I don’t

00:01:28.350 --> 00:01:31.900
want to see New York again on the Upper East
Side?

00:01:31.900 --> 00:01:32.900
Please!”

00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:33.900
Right?

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:36.130
Show me a new world.

00:01:36.130 --> 00:01:42.920
So this is the world of middle and lower middle-class
Atlanta and it’s very (African American

00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:46.469
Atlanta) and it’s very specific and the
story feels very loose.

00:01:46.469 --> 00:01:51.070
And if you go and look at the pilot of Atlanta
and it doesn’t look like it’s about much,

00:01:51.070 --> 00:01:52.740
it’s sort of like a Robert Altman movie.

00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:56.000
It’s got a very loose feel to it.

00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:58.329
It feels very improvisational.

00:01:58.329 --> 00:02:01.750
It feels like there are no rules at all in
this story.

00:02:01.750 --> 00:02:11.390
It’s got every rules, every structure, every
storyline every rhythm of HOW I MEET YOUR

00:02:11.390 --> 00:02:18.830
MOTHER, the most commercial, sit-comey structure
that you’d ever want or you’d ever not

00:02:18.830 --> 00:02:22.820
want or if you’re a hipster you’d say
“I don’t want to write that, right?”

00:02:22.820 --> 00:02:26.970
It follows all the rules, so why is it so
cool.

00:02:26.970 --> 00:02:32.580
Because it takes those rules and it shows
you stuff you’ve never seen.

00:02:32.580 --> 00:02:34.140
The reason people say “Ahhh!

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:40.650
Hollywood is so formulaic is because they
are looking at band, unimaginative stories

00:02:40.650 --> 00:02:46.120
with characters you’ve seen a thousand times,
which situations you’ve been in a thousands

00:02:46.120 --> 00:02:50.860
times, in a world you’ve seen a thousand
times.

00:02:50.860 --> 00:02:58.590
But the structure (the commercial structure)
is there because it’s beautiful, the bones

00:02:58.590 --> 00:02:59.959
are beautiful.

00:02:59.959 --> 00:03:06.690
You can take other bones out, put other bones
in, but there’s an appeal below the surface

00:03:06.690 --> 00:03:07.690
of beauty.

00:03:07.690 --> 00:03:13.950
If you look at what we consider beauty if
you took a skeleton, you’d see that there

00:03:13.950 --> 00:03:16.830
is a conformity to the bones.

00:03:16.830 --> 00:03:20.370
Now you can put whatever you want on that
bone structure.

00:03:20.370 --> 00:03:26.430
You can make the person Tunisian, you can
make them Indian, you can make them American

00:03:26.430 --> 00:03:28.459
Indian, you can make them whatever you want.

00:03:28.459 --> 00:03:32.730
But the bones below that determine whether
or not they are beautiful.

00:03:32.730 --> 00:03:38.959
I think structure of story is like the bones
beneath the face.

00:03:38.959 --> 00:03:46.200
It’s what we’re instructively drawn to
as human beings and that doesn’t change.

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:52.920
But what changes, what makes things unique
is are these characters we haven’t seen

00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:53.920
before.

00:03:53.920 --> 00:03:56.849
Is this guy have a wound we have’t seen
before?

00:03:56.849 --> 00:04:05.890
Glover (in Atlanta) gives us a guy (a real
smart guy) who went to Princeton but he failed

00:04:05.890 --> 00:04:08.890
because he’s got a core wound that he’s
a loser.

00:04:08.890 --> 00:04:14.380
And he’s come back, he’s just bumping
around and hanging out in this middle-class

00:04:14.380 --> 00:04:18.760
(lower middle-class) world of African American
Atlanta but he’s a loser.

00:04:18.760 --> 00:04:24.650
He left Princeton probably because he felt
like these people aren’t like me (which

00:04:24.650 --> 00:04:25.650
I felt.

00:04:25.650 --> 00:04:28.800
I went to an Ivy League school and I felt
the same thing) and because I’m a loser

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:31.450
I’m going to drop out and I’m going to
go back home.

00:04:31.450 --> 00:04:37.610
Now back in Atlanta he gets involved with
his cousin who is a hip hop artist (who he

00:04:37.610 --> 00:04:38.610
shouldn’t get involved with).

00:04:38.610 --> 00:04:45.160
He also already has a baby (Glover does) and
he’s not being a really good father but

00:04:45.160 --> 00:04:46.160
he’s trying.

00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:48.100
He’s not being a good father, he’s not
being a good husband at all.

00:04:48.100 --> 00:04:54.750
So all the things, all of the elements that
we’ve seen from a dozen sitcoms 1960 on,

00:04:54.750 --> 00:04:57.100
are all present in Atlanta.

00:04:57.100 --> 00:04:59.910
But because we’ve never seen this world
before.

00:04:59.910 --> 00:05:06.800
So he’s kind of a slacker (an African American
slacker) and it’s also very funny.

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:13.590
So every commercial cliche of structure is
in this story and yet it’s the freshest

00:05:13.590 --> 00:05:15.480
thing on television.

00:05:15.480 --> 00:05:19.560
And so that’s my argument against not using
structure.

00:05:19.560 --> 00:05:26.110
Think of it this way, in California we have
these vanity license plates, right?

00:05:26.110 --> 00:05:29.850
And in a vanity license plate I think you
get even letters and in that seven letters

00:05:29.850 --> 00:05:33.940
and in that seven letters you’ve got to
be witty.

00:05:33.940 --> 00:05:37.440
You can’t go “Oh man you know what it’s
so cliche to have seven letters.

00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:40.060
My vanity plate is going to have 32.

00:05:40.060 --> 00:05:47.860
Because if you make the vanity plate 32 characters
long, it’s not going to be funny if you

00:05:47.860 --> 00:05:55.070
have a seven letter word that says, let’s
say you’re driving a Corvette and your seven

00:05:55.070 --> 00:05:56.770
letters are “2 Inches.”

00:05:56.770 --> 00:05:57.770
Right?

00:05:57.770 --> 00:05:58.770
That’s funny.

00:05:58.770 --> 00:06:00.440
Film Courage: Yes.

00:06:00.440 --> 00:06:01.440
Got me to laugh!

00:06:01.440 --> 00:06:05.660
Peter: If it was a 42 letter plate plate it
would go “Hey, I drive a Vette because I

00:06:05.660 --> 00:06:07.400
have a small pen**.

00:06:07.400 --> 00:06:08.400
Is that funny?

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:09.400
No!

00:06:09.400 --> 00:06:10.490
Film Courage: No, not as much.

00:06:10.490 --> 00:06:12.400
The form is too big.

00:06:12.400 --> 00:06:16.790
So commercial form is what we delight in.

00:06:16.790 --> 00:06:23.240
The limitations of commercial form produce
the entertainment, right?

00:06:23.240 --> 00:06:28.290
The fact that it’s a half hour means you
must cram certain things in it.

00:06:28.290 --> 00:06:33.370
And the delight of the audience in a commercial
art form is How are they going to cram something

00:06:33.370 --> 00:06:37.770
we’ve never heard about and is witty into
this limited commercial form.

00:06:37.770 --> 00:06:40.310
That’s what commercial art does.

00:06:40.310 --> 00:06:42.480
That’s why it’s commercial art.

00:06:42.480 --> 00:06:47.410
If you want to make a sitcom that doesn’t
follow the rules, great.

00:06:47.410 --> 00:06:50.700
Make one that is four hours long.

00:06:50.700 --> 00:06:56.250
You can do it but it’s not commercial art
and your audience will probably be very limited

00:06:56.250 --> 00:07:00.040
unless you are such a super genius you are
going to create a new art form (that’s what

00:07:00.040 --> 00:07:04.560
Suzy is going to do).

00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:13.360
Please, let’s see it from you but you are
going to have to be greater than Shakespeare

00:07:13.360 --> 00:07:16.530
who buy the way used a three-act structure,
okay?

00:07:16.530 --> 00:07:23.790
Shakespeare worked inside a commercial structure,
the structure of Stratford on Avon.

00:07:23.790 --> 00:07:26.300
He worked inside that structure.

00:07:26.300 --> 00:07:32.630
His plays were inside a commercial structure
and yet they are the greatest plays in history.

00:07:32.630 --> 00:07:35.850
So you can be original and be commercial,
too.

00:07:35.850 --> 00:07:43.180
That’s my seductive message for the whorish
Hollywood that there is a Hollywood pimp telling

00:07:43.180 --> 00:07:44.770
you “C’mon baby.

00:07:44.770 --> 00:07:46.880
Get into the Seraglio.

00:07:46.880 --> 00:07:48.990
Do it our way.

00:07:48.990 --> 00:07:49.759
You can still be original.”

